The Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) is a fundamental concept in software engineering, part of the SOLID principles. It states that a class should have only one reason to change, meaning it should have only one job or responsibility.


Here's a practical example in JavaScript: suppose we have a `User` class that handles both user data and user interface logic. Violating SRP, it might look like this:


```javascript

class User {

  constructor(name, age) {

    this.name = name;

    this.age = age;

  }


  getName() {

    console.log(`User: ${this.name}`);

  }


  getAge() {

    console.log(`User age: ${this.age}`);

  }


  validateName(name) {

    // logic to validate the user's name

  }

}

```


